Wire it through the service's own constructor — declare the dependency as a `private final` field (Java) or a constructor `val` property (Kotlin) and take it as a constructor parameter. Do not add a field-level `@Autowired`, and do not reach for a setter either.

Java:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentClient paymentClient;
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    public OrderService(PaymentClient paymentClient, OrderRepository orderRepository) {
        this.paymentClient = paymentClient;
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
    }
}
```

Note there is no `@Autowired` on the constructor. Since Spring 4.3 a single-constructor class is autowired implicitly, so adding the annotation is redundant noise — if the surrounding codebase does annotate constructors, match that, but the default is to leave it off.

Kotlin:

```kotlin
@Service
class OrderService(
    private val paymentClient: PaymentClient,
    private val orderRepository: OrderRepository,
)
```

Why constructor injection over field `@Autowired`:

- The field can be `final`/`val`, so the dependency cannot be reassigned after construction and the object is never observed in a half-initialized state.
- The dependency graph is visible from the constructor signature instead of hidden across the class body.
- You can instantiate the class in a plain unit test with a mock/stub `PaymentClient` — no Spring context, no reflection-based injection.

A couple of adjacent points that come up with a new `@Service` depending on a client:

- If the paying work is a unit of work, put `@Transactional` on the service method that defines that boundary, not on the class as a grab-bag. And if a method calls another `@Transactional` method via `this`, the annotation will not fire (Spring's proxy is bypassed) — extract that method into a separate bean if it needs its own boundary.
- If `PaymentClient` is itself a Spring bean, just declare the parameter and let the container supply it; if it is an interface with several implementations, resolve ambiguity with `@Qualifier` on the constructor parameter rather than with a field annotation.
- Don't have this service return a JPA `@Entity` from its public methods if a controller will consume the result — map to a DTO at the boundary.

No — that call will not behave as expected if you're relying on the inner method's `@Transactional` annotation to do anything.

Spring's declarative transaction management works through a proxy. When another bean calls a `@Transactional` method, the call goes through the proxy, which opens (or joins) the transaction, invokes your method, and commits/rolls back. But when you call `this.processPayment()` from inside the same class, you're bypassing the proxy entirely — it's just a plain Java method call on the target object, not a call through the proxy. Spring never sees it, so the inner `@Transactional` is silently a no-op.

What this means concretely depends on the propagation you intended:

- If the inner method relies on its own `@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)` to run in a separate transaction, it won't — it'll just run inside whatever transaction the outer method is in (or none, if the outer one has already committed by that point).
- If the inner method relies on its own `@Transactional` (default `REQUIRED`) merely to be transactional at all, and the outer method is *not* itself transactional when this call happens, then the inner work won't be wrapped in a transaction at all.
- If the inner method throws and you expected its own rollback semantics to apply independently, those won't apply either.

The fix is almost always to extract the inner method into a separate Spring bean, constructor-inject that bean, and call it through the injected reference. That way the call goes through the proxy and the annotation fires:

```
// Before: self-invocation, annotation ignored
@Transactional
public void placeOrder(Order order) {
    // ... logic ...
    this.processPayment(order); // bypasses proxy
}

// After: call through an injected bean
private final PaymentService paymentService; // constructor-injected

@Transactional
public void placeOrder(Order order) {
    // ... logic ...
    paymentService.processPayment(order); // goes through the proxy
}
```

A few alternatives if extracting a bean is genuinely awkward, each with caveats: inject a self-reference (`@Lazy` or `ObjectProvider<ThisClass>`) and call through that; use `AopContext.currentProxy()` (requires `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy = true)` and is fragile); or, if the inner method just needs to participate in the outer transaction, make it a plain private method with no annotation and let the outer method's boundary cover it. That last option is often the cleanest when you don't actually need separate propagation — the annotation was only there out of habit.

So: if by "work as expected" you mean "the inner `@Transactional` takes effect," the answer is no. If the goal is simply that the inner work runs inside the outer method's transaction, it happens to work — but only by accident, because of the outer boundary, not because of the inner annotation. That's exactly the kind of thing to make explicit rather than leave to luck.

No — that does not work as expected. This is the classic Spring proxy self-invocation pitfall.

Spring's declarative `@Transactional` is implemented with a proxy (JDK dynamic proxy or CGLIB subclass) that wraps the bean. The annotation is honored only when a call arrives through that proxy — i.e. when the method is invoked from *outside* the bean. When one method inside the class calls another with `this.processPayment()`, the call goes directly against the target instance, completely bypassing the proxy. The interceptor never runs, so the inner `@Transactional` is silently ignored.

Concretely:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(...) {
        // ... does work ...
        this.processPayment(...);   // BUG: proxy bypassed
    }

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(...) {
        // runs with NO transaction semantics of its own
    }
}
```

`placeOrder` is transactional because it was entered through the proxy. But `processPayment` inherits `placeOrder`'s transaction, not its own `@Transactional` settings. If you asked for `REQUIRES_NEW`, `NOT_SUPPORTED`, or a different isolation/timeout, none of that applies. If `placeOrder` had *no* transaction, `processPayment` would run entirely non-transactionally.

Why self-injection and `@Transactional` don't rescue you:
- Injecting the bean into itself (`@Autowired private OrderService self;` then `self.processPayment(...)`) does go through the proxy, but it's circular and ugly; Spring needs `@Lazy` and it obscures intent.
- Annotating the class instead of the method doesn't help — the proxy bypass is the issue, not the annotation's location.
- The same trap hits `@Async`, `@Cacheable`, `@PreAuthorize`, `@Retryable`, etc. — all are proxy-based.

Correct fixes, in order of preference:

1. **Extract the inner method into a separate, constructor-injected bean.** This is the clean fix and keeps the transaction boundary explicit:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessor {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(...) { ... }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor;

    public OrderService(PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor) {
        this.paymentProcessor = paymentProcessor;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(...) {
        paymentProcessor.processPayment(...);  // goes through the proxy
    }
}
```

2. If the call genuinely must stay in one class and you need the separate transaction semantics, use `TransactionTemplate` and run the inner work inside it — no proxy involved, so no bypass:

```java
private final TransactionTemplate requiresNew;

public void placeOrder(...) {
    // ...
    requiresNew.execute(status -> { doProcessPayment(...); return null; });
}
```

3. Self-injection is a last resort; usable but circular and hard to read.

One more note: even when the transaction is correctly applied, `REQUIRES_NEW` on the inner call means the outer transaction is suspended and a second DB connection is held for the duration — worth checking the pool can afford it under load.

Given the choice in this exercise, the extraction-into-a-separate-bean fix (option 1) is the one to reach for: it makes the dependency and the transaction boundary visible in the class's own constructor, which is exactly the property you lose when you hide the call behind `this`.

No — that does not work as expected. This is the classic self-invocation pitfall with Spring's proxy-based declarative transactions.

**Why it fails**

Spring applies `@Transactional` by wrapping the bean in a proxy (a CGLIB subclass or JDK dynamic proxy) at bean-creation time. When an external caller invokes a method on the injected bean reference, the call goes *through the proxy*, and the proxy starts/joins the transaction around the target method before delegating.

But when you write `this.processPayment()` inside the same class, you are calling the method directly on the target object, not through the proxy. The proxy is bypassed entirely, so its transaction interceptor never runs. The inner `@Transactional` annotation is silently a no-op: no new transaction is started, no `REQUIRED`/`REQUIRES_NEW` propagation is applied, and no rollback-only/commit semantics specific to that inner method take effect. It just runs as an ordinary method call inside whatever transaction (or none) the outer method already established.

There is no error, no warning, and no log line. That silence is what makes it dangerous: the code looks correct and often passes happy-path tests, because in the common case the outer transaction happens to be open anyway. The bug only surfaces when the inner method's intended transaction semantics actually matter.

**Concrete failure modes**

- If `processPayment` is marked `REQUIRES_NEW` (say, to commit the payment record even if the surrounding order transaction later rolls back), the `REQUIRES_NEW` is ignored. The payment commits or rolls back with the *outer* transaction instead — the opposite of the intended behavior.
- If the outer method is *not* transactional but calls `this.processPayment()` expecting the inner `@Transactional` to provide one, no transaction exists at all — writes may auto-commit per statement, with no atomicity.
- If the inner method is meant to be `readOnly = true` while the outer is read-write, that distinction is lost.
- Rollback rules configured on the inner method (`rollbackFor`, `noRollbackFor`, `readOnly`) never apply.

**The fix**

Move `processPayment` into a separate, constructor-injected bean so the call crosses a proxy boundary:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(Payment p) { ... }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
        this.paymentService = paymentService;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order o) {
        // ...
        paymentService.processPayment(...); // goes through the proxy
    }
}
```

This is why the skill's Rule is unconditional: *never call another `@Transactional` method on `this` and rely on the annotation firing.* A new `@Transactional` method that another method in the same class needs to invoke is a design signal that the two methods belong in different beans, or that the transaction boundary should be redrawn.

**Alternatives, and when they are acceptable**

- **Self-injection** (`@Autowired private OrderService self;` then `self.processPayment(...)`) works but is widely considered a code smell and can cause circular-dependency issues on older Spring versions. Prefer extracting a bean.
- **`AopContext.currentProxy()`** requires `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy = true)` and couples the domain code to Spring AOP internals. Also a smell.
- **`TransactionTemplate`** — if you genuinely need programmatic control within one class, injecting a `TransactionTemplate` and calling `template.execute(...)` is explicit and correct, though usually the extracted-bean approach is cleaner.

**Verification before you call it done**

- Confirm no method in the changed class invokes another `@Transactional` method of the same class through `this`.
- Confirm the intended propagation (especially `REQUIRES_NEW` vs. `REQUIRED`) is actually observable — ideally with a test that asserts the rollback/commit behavior on the inner unit of work, since happy-path tests will not catch a bypassed annotation.
- In Kotlin the trap is identical: `this.processPayment()` and `processPayment()` both bypass the proxy; only a call on a separate injected bean reference crosses it.

No — that is exactly the self-invocation pitfall the skill calls out, and no, it does not work as expected.

Spring's declarative transaction support (`@Transactional`) is **proxy-based**. When an external caller invokes a bean method, it goes through the Spring-generated proxy, which starts/joins a transaction based on the annotation, then delegates to your actual method. But a call to `this.processPayment()` from *inside* another method of the same class never goes through the proxy — it's a plain Java/Kotlin method call on the raw target object. So whatever `@Transactional` settings you put on `processPayment` (propagation, isolation, readOnly, rollback rules) are **silently ignored**. It just runs inline in whatever transaction context the *calling* method already established.

Concrete consequences:

- If `processPayment` was annotated `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)`, that new transaction never starts — it silently joins (or runs inside) the outer one.
- If `processPayment` was annotated `@Transactional` and the caller was **not** transactional, then no transaction is started at all for that inner work — despite the annotation.
- If `processPayment` throws and is expected to roll back only its own unit of work, that rollback boundary does not exist; the outer transaction decides everything.

The fix is to move the method to a **separate, constructor-injected bean**. Then the call goes through the proxy and the annotation takes effect:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor;   // separate bean

    public OrderService(PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor) {
        this.paymentProcessor = paymentProcessor;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        // ...
        paymentProcessor.processPayment(req);  // goes through the proxy now
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessor {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(OrderRequest req) {
        // new transaction actually starts here
    }
}
```

Alternatives: inject the bean into itself (`@Lazy self`), or use `AopContext.currentProxy()` — but the recommended and cleanest fix per this project's rules is extracting it into a separate, constructor-injected collaborator, which also keeps responsibilities narrow and the dependency graph explicit.

So: if you see `this.processPayment()` inside another `@Transactional` method and you're relying on the inner annotation to do something (new transaction, its own rollback, its own isolation), that reliance is a bug. Either it's a no-op you didn't intend, or the inner annotation was redundant all along — either way the code is misleading and should be refactored so the call crosses a proxy boundary.

No — that does **not** work as expected.

Spring's declarative `@Transactional` is implemented via a dynamic proxy that wraps the bean. The proxy is what opens (and commits/rolls back) the transaction when an *external* caller invokes a proxied method. When you call `this.processPayment()` from inside another method of the same class, you are invoking the method directly on the raw target object, completely bypassing the proxy. The proxied call never happens, so the inner method's `@Transactional` is silently ignored.

Concrete consequences, depending on how the annotations are configured:

- If `processPayment` declares `@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)` expecting a **new** transaction independent of the caller, it will instead just participate in the caller's existing transaction (or run with whatever transaction the *outer* method established). No new transaction is started.
- If `processPayment` declares `@Transactional(rollbackFor = ...)` or a different isolation/read-only setting expecting different semantics than the caller, none of it takes effect — the inner method runs under the outer method's transaction attributes.
- If `processPayment` is the *only* transactional method and the outer method isn't transactional at all, then nothing is transactional and you get no transaction where you intended one.
- If `processPayment` throws an exception expecting a rollback scoped to its own work, that rollback boundary doesn't exist; the failure propagates into the caller's transaction (or none).

**How to fix it** — extract the inner method into a separate, constructor-injected bean so the call goes through that bean's proxy:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessor {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void processPayment(Payment p) { ... }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentProcessor paymentProcessor; // constructor-injected

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order o) {
        // ...
        paymentProcessor.processPayment(p); // goes through the proxy -> works
    }
}
```

Alternatives, in order of preference:

1. **Extract to a separate bean** (above) — cleanest, self-documenting, testable.
2. **Self-injection** — inject the bean into itself and call `self.processPayment(...)`. Works, but is a smell that usually points at a layering problem; reach for this only when extraction is genuinely impractical.
3. **`AopContext.currentProxy()`** with `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy = true)` — requires configuration and a cast; brittle and generally discouraged.
4. **`TransactionTemplate`** — replace the annotation with an explicit programmatic transaction around the inner call. Fine if you want the boundary explicit, but it trades declarative clarity for imperative code.

One caution: reflection-based tests (or unit tests that instantiate the class with `new` rather than from the Spring context) will **not** catch this, because they call the method directly just like `this` does — the bug only manifests at runtime through the real proxy. That's exactly why it's worth verifying against an integration test that fetches the bean from the application context, or simply avoiding same-class transactional calls altogether.

Don't reach for `Thread.sleep(...)` — it's the classic flaky-test trap: too short and it fails under load, too long and the suite crawls, and either way it's nondeterministic. In a Spring Boot test you have three good options, in rough order of preference.

## 1. Awaitility (use by default)

Add `org.awaitility:awaitility` (test scope) and write:

```java
import static org.awaitility.Awaitility.await;
import static java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit.SECONDS;

@Test
void processesOrderAsynchronously() {
    orderService.placeOrder(order);

    await().atMost(5, SECONDS)
           .untilAsserted(() -> {
               Order saved = orderRepository.findById(order.getId()).orElseThrow();
               assertThat(saved.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PROCESSED);
           });
}
```

Key points:
- `untilAsserted(...)` re-runs the whole assertion block on a poll interval (default 100ms / 500ms), so a race between the condition becoming true and you reading it disappears.
- Assert on the *observable end state* (a DB row, a captured mock, a field), not on an internal flag.
- Prefer `untilAsserted` over `until(() -> someGetter() != null)` — the latter is prone to NPEs mid-flight; the former swallows exceptions until the timeout.
- If the project uses Kotlin/MockK, the syntax is identical — it's a Java library, import it the same way.

## 2. Have the async method return a `Future`/`CompletableFuture`

If the method under test is `@Async` and returns `CompletableFuture<T>`, just join it:

```java
CompletableFuture<Receipt> result = orderService.placeOrderAsync(order);
Receipt receipt = result.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS); // or .join()
assertThat(receipt.getStatus()).isEqualTo(PROCESSED);
```

This is the cleanest option *when it's available* — no polling, fully deterministic. It only works if the method's signature gives you something to wait on, which is often a reason to prefer a `CompletableFuture`-returning signature in the design.

## 3. `CountDownLatch`

Useful when the async side has no return value and no queryable end state — e.g. it fires a callback:

```java
CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
doAnswer(inv -> { latch.countDown(); return null; })
    .when(listener).onProcessed(any());

orderService.placeOrder(order);

assertThat(latch.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)).isTrue();
```

This is more manual and easier to get wrong than Awaitility, so reach for it only when you're verifying a *callback was invoked* rather than an end state you can poll.

## Two gotchas specific to `@Async` in tests

**The executor must actually exist and be async.** If your test is a plain unit test (`@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)`) that constructs the service directly, `@Async` does nothing — there's no Spring proxy, so the method runs synchronously on the calling thread and your wait passes trivially for the wrong reason. Verify the async behavior in a `@SpringBootTest` (or a targeted slice that wires the proxy), or assert on the task executor directly.

**Same-class self-invocation breaks `@Async`.** If the async work is triggered by a method calling another method on `this`, the proxy is bypassed and it runs synchronously — again making the test pass without testing anything. If you suspect that, add a quick assertion that the caller thread differs from the worker thread (`assertThat(Thread.currentThread().getName()).doesNotContain("task-")` inside the async body, or capture `Thread.currentThread()` in a mock and assert on it).

## What to avoid

- `Thread.sleep(...)` / Kotlin `delay(...)` — nondeterministic under CI load.
- Retrying a flaky test in CI config to paper over a missing join/await — that hides a real determinism bug rather than fixing it.
- Polling with a manual `while (System.currentTimeMillis() < deadline)` loop — reinventing Awaitility, worse.

Default to Awaitility with `untilAsserted`; switch to a `Future.get`/`join` if the method already returns one, since that's strictly more deterministic.

Don't use `Thread.sleep` — it's the classic source of flaky tests (too short and it fails under load, too long and the suite crawls). Use one of these instead, in rough order of preference:

**1. Awaitility (best for an `@Async` void method)**

```java
@Test
void sendsEmailAfterOrderPlaced() {
    orderService.placeOrder(order);   // fires the @Async method

    await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
           .untilAsserted(() -> then(emailClient).should().send(order.getEmail()));

    verify(emailClient).send(order.getEmail());
}
```

`untilAsserted` polls the assertion itself, so it passes as soon as the work is done and fails deterministically if it never happens. This is the go-to when the async method returns `void` and you're observing a side effect on a mock/collaborator.

**2. Return a `CompletableFuture`/`Future` and join it (best when you can)**

```java
@Async
CompletableFuture<Receipt> process(Order order) { ... }

@Test
void processesOrder() throws Exception {
    CompletableFuture<Receipt> future = orderService.process(order);
    Receipt receipt = future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    assertThat(receipt.status()).isEqualTo(COMPLETED);
}
```

If you control the `@Async` method's signature, prefer this — no polling at all, it just blocks until done. (A `Future.get(timeout)` is also what you want if the service is being called through a proxy.)

**3. `CountDownLatch` for callback-style async**

```java
CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
doAnswer(inv -> { latch.countDown(); return null; })
    .when(listener).onComplete(any());

orderService.placeOrder(order);
assertThat(latch.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)).isTrue();
```

Use this when the completion signal is a listener/callback rather than a return value.

**Mockito's `timeout()` verify, as a lighter alternative to Awaitility**

```java
verify(emailClient, timeout(5000)).send(order.getEmail());
```

Handy for the simple "assert it was eventually called" case, but Awaitility is more expressive when the assertion is richer than a single call.

**Two gotchas specific to `@Async` in tests:**

- **Without `@EnableAsync`, the `@Async` method runs synchronously** on the calling thread — so any of the above "just works" but you're not actually testing the async behavior. Make sure the async config is active in the test context if you care about that path.
- **Self-invocation defeats the proxy** — if the test calls a method that internally calls the `@Async` method on `this`, it runs synchronously. The async behavior only kicks in via the Spring proxy (an external call or a call on the injected bean).

My recommendation: if you can change the signature, have the `@Async` method return `CompletableFuture<T>` and `get()` with a timeout — it's the most robust and needs no polling library. If it's `void` and you're verifying a side effect, use Awaitility's `untilAsserted`. Either way, always bound the wait with a timeout so a regression fails fast instead of hanging CI.

Use Awaitility (or an explicit join/latch) — never `Thread.sleep`. Awaitility is the standard answer in this skill: `await().atMost(...).until(...)` polls until the condition holds or fails deterministically, so the test can't flake under load the way a fixed sleep does.

The three acceptable patterns:

**1. Awaitility (usually the best fit).** If your async method's only observable effect is on some state (a mocked collaborator was called, a record was written, a counter was incremented), poll that condition:

```java
@Test
void sendsConfirmationEmailAfterOrderPlaced() {
    orderService.placeOrder(order);

    await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
           .untilAsserted(() -> verify(emailSender).sendConfirmation(order));
}
```

Don't `verify` immediately after `placeOrder` returns — the `@Async` proxied method has only been submitted to the executor at that point, not necessarily run.

**2. Join a `CompletableFuture`.** If the method under test returns a `CompletableFuture`/`Future`, join it directly — that's deterministic and needs no polling:

```java
CompletableFuture<Void> future = orderService.placeOrderAsync(order);
future.join();   // or future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
verify(emailSender).sendConfirmation(order);
```

**3. `CountDownLatch`.** If you're testing the async method in isolation and it has no return value and no collaborator to observe, have the test drive a latch:

```java
CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
when(emailSender.sendConfirmation(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> { latch.countDown(); return null; });

orderService.placeOrder(order);
assertThat(latch.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)).isTrue();
```

A few Spring-specific things to check so the async actually runs in the test:

- **`@EnableAsync` must be on the context you load.** In a plain unit test (no Spring container), `@Async` is not honored at all — the method runs synchronously, because there's no proxy. That's often the cleanest path: test the async method's logic synchronously by constructing the class directly, and cover the `@Async` wiring separately if it matters.
- **Testing the real async behavior requires Spring to proxy the bean.** So it needs a Spring test slice/slice with the async config present, and you must call the method through the injected proxy (not `new` it yourself), or the async annotation is a no-op.
- **Don't use a test-only `SyncTaskExecutor` and then also poll** — if you replace the executor with a synchronous one, the call already completed when the method returns, and the wait is pointless. Pick one: either a real async executor + Awaitility/join, or a synchronous executor + assert directly.

The red flag to avoid is `Thread.sleep(500)` "to let the async finish" — it's non-deterministic under CI load and hides the real question of whether the async work is actually observable when you need it to be.

No — for a controller test, use `@WebMvcTest`, not `@SpringBootTest`.

`@SpringBootTest` boots the entire application context, which is the slowest option and buys you nothing here: you're testing request/response mapping, status codes, and validation on the controller, and the service layer should be mocked anyway.

Concretely, for an `OrderController` that calls `OrderService`:

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @MockBean
    private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void getOrder_returnsOrder() throws Exception {
        Order order = new Order(1L, "PLACED");
        when(orderService.getOrder(1L)).thenReturn(order);

        mockMvc.perform(get("/orders/1"))
               .andExpect(status().isOk())
               .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1L))
               .andExpect(jsonPath("$.status").value("PLACED"));
    }

    @Test
    void getOrder_unknownId_returns404() throws Exception {
        when(orderService.getOrder(99L)).thenThrow(new OrderNotFoundException(99L));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/orders/99"))
               .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }
}
```

Kotlin equivalent uses `@MockkBean` with MockK instead of `@MockBean`.

A few things to check before you write it, per the skill's Step 1:

1. **Read a neighboring test class first.** If this project already has controller tests, copy their slice and assertion style (`MockMvc` vs `WebTestClient` if it's WebFlux, AssertJ vs Hamcrest jsonPath). Don't introduce a different convention.
2. **Check the validation path.** If the controller takes a `@Valid @RequestBody`, add a case asserting the Bean Validation failure returns the expected 400 — that's one of the cases this slice exists to cover.
3. **Mock only the direct collaborator.** `@MockBean OrderService` in the slice; if the controller also injects a mapper or validator bean, mock those too, otherwise the context slice won't start.

Where `@SpringBootTest` *would* be justified is if the thing under test is genuinely the wiring across layers — e.g. you want to prove the controller, service, and persistence all interact correctly end to end. That's a different test with a different purpose; it doesn't replace this controller test, and it isn't the default.

If you also need coverage for the service's own logic, write a plain unit test that constructs `OrderService` directly with Mockito mocks passed to its constructor — no Spring container at all. And if there's repository logic behind this (a query that might N+1), that's a `@DataJpaTest`, using Testcontainers if the project already has it wired rather than swapping in H2.

Short answer: no, not by default. For a new `@RestController` whose job is request/response mapping, status codes, and validation errors, use `@WebMvcTest` with the service mocked via `@MockBean`. `@SpringBootTest` loads the whole application context to test the web layer, which is slower and tests more than what a controller test needs.

Here's how the decision works per what's under test:

- **`@WebMvcTest`** — the controller itself. Loads only the web slice (MVC, Jackson, Bean Validation). Use `MockMvc` to assert the HTTP status, the response body shape, and that a validation failure returns the expected 400. Mock the service with `@MockBean` (or `@MockitoBean` on newer Spring).
- **Plain unit test, no Spring** — if the "service" is your own class with constructor injection, test its business logic directly with Mockito/MockK mocks passed to the constructor. This is often what people actually need when they reach for `@SpringBootTest`.
- **`@DataJpaTest`** — the repository, if the service call bottoms out in a query you need to verify.
- **`@SpringBootTest`** — only when the cross-cutting wired interaction of the whole context is genuinely what you're testing (e.g. an end-to-end flow through several real beans). It's the slowest option and shouldn't be the default.

